SCALE_REALISM: "Partly plausible but not yet demonstrated at realistic workload. The L0 artifact is reproducible-looking and uses the same bounded-buffer mechanic as tf.data/WebDataset/HF, but evidence is synthetic: fixed corr=0.80, source-id signatures, N up to 256k, b=32, and no measured source run lengths or gradient correlations from a real corpus. The packet asserts practitioner B~10-50b and L>=B without cited deployment data. vLLM/SGLang/LMCache/FlashInfer/TensorRT-LLM are inference/KV-cache systems and I found no relevant shipping data-loader shuffle mechanism in their README-level surfaces; the relevant OSS prior art is data pipeline systems above."
RATIONALE: "No red: I did not find a shipping system or paper that already states the exact B/L variance-inflation ceiling with the packet's grid metric. However, this is not green because the mechanism itself is already widely shipped, and several systems already expose the operational answer: tf.data requires cardinality-sized buffers for uniform full shuffle; HF streaming shuffles shards plus a finite buffer; Grain recommends global shuffle when random access exists and hierarchical/window shuffle otherwise; Mosaic explicitly ties shuffle quality to loss variance/spikes and advises stronger shuffle parameters. The claimed contribution is therefore an empirical/analytic insight about sizing against run length, not a new systems design. The reported L0 numbers are strong within the synthetic harness, e.g. 15.10x inflation at B=10b,L=20b and 2.17x still at B=10L, but realistic-scale validity and actionability remain insufficiently evidenced. Next map delta should mark this as candidate-level insight requiring L1 real-corpus measurements and comparison against existing mitigations: shard interleaving, source mixing/stratified batching, Grain global shuffle, and Mosaic shuffle_block_size/num_canonical_nodes tuning."
